# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine cloned.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, which converts every text
# file to CRLF on checkout. For Rust sources that is invisible. For the shell
# scripts under `packaging/` it is not: a `#!/bin/sh` script with CRLF endings
# fails on Linux with a message naming an interpreter that does not exist,
# because the carriage return is part of the path it tried to run.
#
# **This was measured on the first Windows checkout of this repository**, on
# 2026-09-04, and every one of `build-deb.sh`, `install.sh`, `check-libraries.sh`
# and `version.sh` had been checked out with CRLF. Nothing in the repository was
# wrong - `core.autocrlf` converts back on the way in, so what is stored is LF
# either way - and nothing had failed yet, because those four are Linux scripts
# that no Windows lane runs. The Linux lane would have found it the first time
# somebody cloned there from a Windows push.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the repository and checks out LF
# everywhere, so a file written on Windows is a file that still runs on Debian.
# This is slipcase-desktop's file and its reasoning; this repository went four
# stages without one because it had only ever been cloned on Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The templated files. `control.in` becomes a member of a Debian archive and the
# two manual pages become roff, and both formats are line-oriented with no CRLF
# in them.
*.in text eol=lf

# Not text, whatever a heuristic makes of the contents. The `.ico` files and the
# Store's PNGs are committed artifacts - `packaging/windows/README.md` says why -
# and a newline conversion applied to one is a corrupted icon that nothing would
# report.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.deb binary
*.dclx binary
